#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134812} :activity/id #uuid "e5c7e80a-2ee4-5b4a-a66d-c98773047176" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051085 :topic/id #uuid "7fdf89be-7454-5a23-9d34-17c62e869eea" :topic/math-academy-id 2132} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135103 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3,$ expressed in terms of its component functions as\n\n$$\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k.$$\n\nIf the first partial derivatives of $P, Q,$ and $R$ all exist, then the **curl** of $\\mathbf F,$ denoted $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F,$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ given by\n\n$$\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial z} \\right)\\,\\mathbf i + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial z} - \\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial x}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k.$$\n\nThe formula above is pretty tough to remember. Luckily, there is an easier way. If we define the differential operator $\\nabla$ (a.k.a. the \"del\" operator) as\n\n$$\\nabla = \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} + \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y} + \\mathbf k\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial z}$$\n\nthen the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is given by the cross product\n\n$$\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\times\\mathbf F.$$\n\nExpressing the quantity on the right-hand side as a determinant, we have\n\n$$\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\times\\mathbf F = \\left| \\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned} \\right|.$$\n\nFor example, suppose that the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=xz\\,\\mathbf{i}+ xy\\,\\mathbf{j}+yz\\,\\mathbf{k}.$$\n\nThen we can calculate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ using the determinant formula as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ xz & xy & yz\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)]k \\\\ & =(z-0)\\,i-(0-x)\\,j+(y-0)\\,k \\\\ & =z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "748e0ff5-caab-5725-a53e-a87f3322d0b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5141 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cd896bf6-f61b-5f98-b1ed-40e9aa1214a5" :step/math-academy-id 32355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133693 :question/id #uuid "4e4236a9-d99f-50b1-803a-ab3aea5a673e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8743"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad4e453-00da-5c77-8d6a-5de4dc3d5593" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385913}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Curl of a Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "ed910a8f-f9f5-5744-a048-0db164b576a3" :step/math-academy-id 32563 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134874}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133697 :question/id #uuid "cb99a3e1-9cd1-5335-b20c-01ad598d14bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-8929"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446573}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "9c1c4ab6-b310-57fe-81bc-08a0edb50c57" :step/math-academy-id 33327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135115 :tutorial/content "We know how to compute the curl of a vector field. Our goal now is to develop an intuitive understanding of what the curl of a vector field measures.\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ shown below. Assume that $\\mathbf F$ is independent of $z.$ \n\n![](images/13/13db0656977bb206ccca66fd25fee08ee2b3c8724173b74aeb4e856109ce0be5.png)\n\nIt's helpful to imagine that this vector field represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. \n\nNow, we pick an arbitrary point in the $xy$-plane and place a paddlewheel at our point with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction (the direction of the curl).\n\n![](images/0e/0eb70168474799d1b94311f444aef72e1904b0c8e13042ba1ba182cd23e57dba.png)\n\nThe curl of the vector field at our point measures how much the paddlewheel rotates due to the action of the water. There are two possible cases, illustrated below.\n\n![](images/86/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl. In other words, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\mathbf 0.$ In this case, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is **irrotational**.\n\nFor the particular point we've chosen in our example, we can see that the action of the water will cause the paddleboard to rotate counterclockwise.\n\n![](images/96/96bfb9c08d2d5ea0c119e8e4ee6a54e8aaa9fee0eabe8fb13b7c65cc586c3094.png)\n\nSince the water causes the paddleboard to rotate, we must have $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0.$\n\nFinally, note that if $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F =\\mathbf 0,$ then it does not point in any particular direction." :tutorial/id #uuid "b69ffb3c-f0ed-59d1-b022-ccb162de3d67" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5519 :tutorial/title "Physical Interpretation of the Curl"} :step/id #uuid "b31fa9fe-8595-5f98-bdde-8a55d53eccb1" :step/math-academy-id 34360 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135117 :tutorial/content "We can only compute the curl of a *vector* field, and a vector field's curl is also a vector field. So if $\\mathbf G = \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F,$ then we have the following map:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}} \\mapsto\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf G(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}}\n$$\n\nIn contrast, if $f(x,y, z)$ is a scalar field, then the statement $\\textrm{curl}\\, f$ has no meaning.\n\nThe curl of a vector field at a point $P$ gives us helpful information about the rotational behavior of the vector field at $P.$ We'll describe this in more detail shortly." :tutorial/id #uuid "c5a7bd6a-0498-53af-afe7-1a9974db120d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5789 :tutorial/title "Some Notes About the Curl of a Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "a02eaa37-2a1d-55e1-98e9-5eda118d61d2" :step/math-academy-id 35528 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134823}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135118 :tutorial/content "In general, when we compute the curl of a vector field at a point, the result is a vector. This vector gives us two pieces of information about the rotation of a paddleboard placed at that point:\n\n- The magnitude of the curl vector indicates the speed of the rotation of the paddleboard.\n\n- The direction of the curl vector gives us the *axis of rotation* on which the paddlewheel should be placed for maximum rotation.\n\nThe orientation of the curl vector is determined by the so-called **right-hand rule**. To understand this, let's return to our example:\n\n![](images/96/96bfb9c08d2d5ea0c119e8e4ee6a54e8aaa9fee0eabe8fb13b7c65cc586c3094.png)\n\nUsing our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point *away* from the screen. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$ \n\nThe situation is reversed when the rotation of the vector field is clockwise:\n\n![](images/4b/4b13f2a96f05124184ad8b7f2007b2267a55679b55e5ed6344a2609696abe0be.png)\n\nUsing our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point *into* the screen. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4456c748-e7a9-5576-b733-c6033799f9f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5790 :tutorial/title "The Right-Hand Rule"} :step/id #uuid "7edf68a9-c5bd-5bbc-82e9-a0291639597e" :step/math-academy-id 35529 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135119 :tutorial/content "The following two vector fields would both cause a paddleboard to rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, their curl is nonzero.\n\n![](images/92/92654626dae292c06a227622c6eb590df6e7c898eebe1c06d2b3cd57630dfea0.png)\n\nThe following two vector fields would both cause a paddleboard to rotate clockwise. Therefore, their curl is nonzero.\n\n![](images/2c/2cda9e8468dff1fdfe3d60c4069054f1f85ea4c6356075b576f69228def7496c.png)\n\nThe following vector fields are irrotational (i.e., they each have zero curl).\n\n![](images/fd/fd66b65e3b9bdba852e9bfa2826f3950d4b901cab2c76ce0a153491daf338b21.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "83e8ceb0-2e0b-502b-bfd5-8571ac38410c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5792 :tutorial/title "Examples of Vector Fields With Curl and Irrotational Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "48e5d042-5f59-5eae-8bb5-54d78cbde685" :step/math-academy-id 35534 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133898 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133714 :question/id #uuid "39577555-e9cb-563a-8f5e-8b9ea4b57364" :question/math-academy-id "e-9556"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446574}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "7ee830f5-6150-5cbf-b49c-f6582be4799a" :step/math-academy-id 35806 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135135 :tutorial/content "Technically, we can only find the curl of a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ then (strictly speaking) $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ has no meaning. \n\nHowever, it's sometimes helpful to avoid this issue by considering $\\mathbf F$ as a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ whose $\\mathbf k$ component is zero everywhere. \n\nAssuming that the $\\mathbf k$ component of $\\mathbf F$ is zero everywhere, the curl must point in the $z$-direction perpendicular to your screen. To see why, note that if we start from\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k,\n$$\n\nthen set \n\n$$\nR(x,y,z) = 0, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial z}= \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial z} = 0\n$$\n\nand, finally, substitute the above into our curl formula, then we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \n= \n\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nNote that the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial y}\n$$\n\n \nis called the **scalar curl** of $\\mathbf F.$ The scalar curl is associated with two-dimensional vector fields only." :tutorial/id #uuid "07a27175-fb76-5663-98dd-203ead020fc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5848 :tutorial/title "The Curl of a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "2f58e72d-0cd5-5224-8856-908ecd564b96" :step/math-academy-id 35822 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134869}}] :activity/title "The Curl of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134011}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409612} :activity/id #uuid "e5b5f674-7783-5eb4-b089-af373cffcaf2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051085 :topic/id #uuid "7fdf89be-7454-5a23-9d34-17c62e869eea" :topic/math-academy-id 2132} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8286c6dd-7fea-5018-b722-69a0848b0bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437502} {:answer/id #uuid "5d52abdf-7ac5-55e3-92ae-9aec71a85bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437502} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb53bf33-5552-5395-8fd4-4d42b50a2a01" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4939bd9-e529-5af3-b533-59e09598d0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437504} {:answer/id #uuid "2b9d89e5-98a9-5520-8de9-5ddb178c0664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437505}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437505} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a596bab-34c8-5ee7-9e82-c47a2988e863" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "273314f4-9ed1-52ac-8d9b-6701a93d8748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437506} {:answer/id #uuid "265725b9-3d43-5f84-a380-552591b33792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437507} :answer-field/id #uuid "05fd84cc-0dd3-5f03-9595-e86a0cb51c38" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438782} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fc09d16-5459-5df8-9d7e-c5304315ffbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437508} {:answer/id #uuid "3423417a-d67c-5bac-8ed6-720f61a5aa60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437508} :answer-field/id #uuid "31b1fd93-e1ee-539e-8481-cca67e61611b" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "656969c6-847a-533c-a9b8-d526f82e1ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-105694" :question/problem "![](images/17/17637e938a25e91b9a03921dbbe0f22c8ea760f09cfed9b4a76d07bd5e3b9301.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- **III.** $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n- **IV.** $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/f3/f385add60afac473a5734f8c1ed57c6575bada03c9c16b24fed73b200c38e2c5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3e935ab0-b71e-5b6d-b820-ffbfd12a9752" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecf86ba1-b225-5f4e-a85f-182bd2bdbc53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437478} {:answer/id #uuid "c7744411-0aa5-54ef-9c74-d6ca7e53bdb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437479} :answer-field/id #uuid "af1fc1b7-1f6d-5789-bbaf-748a9803bc96" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438768} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9168e608-76cb-5350-991f-3427546bf922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437480} {:answer/id #uuid "6acf104d-e9d0-56e0-a85c-9b1ece39cee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437480} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa0313b-ee82-5436-b649-0b4504bba2d2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf15f6c8-cfc5-5bbe-886a-d7a4c35fe3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437482} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab85c7d-e507-5369-a231-49fca8b2c436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437483} :answer-field/id #uuid "c223aec0-697d-56a5-825e-44718ec7f2d3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4d8d559-74b6-5016-bfae-9833564ce138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437484} {:answer/id #uuid "ee14db71-cbc6-5c05-87b0-f9afe0453fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437484} :answer-field/id #uuid "28f80fe3-8688-556e-8b02-a42b1e403d0f" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "54529993-0bd1-511f-b362-f0eafc672ef8" :question/math-academy-id "q-105671" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c38294780ac6d5079b759f39a7dbd4944b1c3c21495245dfd314ec180c0434e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- **III.** $F$ is irrotational\n- **IV.** $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/97/979b63f21e56924bd7880f9487cf829089e38cf14d287e70196c7a661e41f97f.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9367b403-efcf-5ce5-86fa-9fe85e17890b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409611}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd5ff55-9267-592c-8f53-54d27c28ddbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409591} {:answer/id #uuid "64ee45f8-90e8-584e-9ee0-f763b1794d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409596} {:answer/id #uuid "81f3041d-6679-5b2e-8990-3525e4b2226c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409598} {:answer/id #uuid "9811b973-e6d5-574c-9870-5a2db507cfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409600} {:answer/id #uuid "d498b33f-c852-5e73-bfc6-bc624cb1045e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4xy}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409591} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d405633-3d03-5b83-b9d8-c7d039eaf05b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5a957b95-e78d-5791-8e30-44fc756d4a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131343" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "94cf6f34-eb3b-582a-a36f-1fccec5bf9a0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "613b73ad-b4bd-580f-91d8-dabafde0761e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409595} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf92fb5-00f1-572a-8207-d72045cf4a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409599} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5d28d8-413b-530d-ad6e-efa5df8905f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409601} {:answer/id #uuid "ee35c795-c099-5c49-977b-052171747158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409603} {:answer/id #uuid "f7113576-7a9c-589a-960b-40c8d014ca07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409601} :answer-field/id #uuid "9898eac0-330a-51e3-bb68-1c7f9a46f935" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "494f0061-8555-5747-be24-aea11c4501ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-126428" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(\\pi ,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(\\pi ,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(\\pi ,0)} & =-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0-\\pi \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b9f70b49-6fa6-5fee-a84d-6cdc4f073bd2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13264dd3-32ca-5425-ae4a-10ae592d25f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260044} {:answer/id #uuid "1ad4275e-e84f-5e99-9926-6ed1c4bda7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec4d041-01f8-5cba-bbc5-711d818eb654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260692} {:answer/id #uuid "7344edf5-4fa2-53cc-aefd-2082b97c5134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260883} {:answer/id #uuid "deb142df-e54d-5f60-a501-f62bc2ccccc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260108} :answer-field/id #uuid "6208b069-a1cc-5b39-96a3-5295dfbd12c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "84980841-460b-507f-8790-439efc8ff63f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126525" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,i+(x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(0,\\pi /2)} & ={(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e05e6b0f-4f4c-5280-957e-b076cba38cb7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409613}}] :activity/title "The Curl of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409590}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481728} :activity/id #uuid "0fc222ec-8ee3-576a-8975-559e2c61b885" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051085 :topic/id #uuid "7fdf89be-7454-5a23-9d34-17c62e869eea" :topic/math-academy-id 2132} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77742b04-e958-51e3-885d-c186ce960ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437856} {:answer/id #uuid "7a46d360-0b63-59bd-8d4c-b0aa9e72d4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437857}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437856} :answer-field/id #uuid "24781376-5a9f-5184-b064-24b3c476bb79" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d33cbee7-fc8b-5c3d-bfba-41b228bb82bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437858} {:answer/id #uuid "923f7469-7fef-5157-a155-0ff72c5145be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437859} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb64428-9f16-5737-8c74-c42a032a0297" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438958} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058fa0b3-ef79-5a7a-afc6-113a760e3e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437860} {:answer/id #uuid "5292bd0e-9d53-564c-bffc-65c61607f30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437861} :answer-field/id #uuid "52c67e92-c96c-5d8b-b9f6-cc9263317637" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438959} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6613a08-4056-5a9f-9ca6-c3c5e4078896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437862} {:answer/id #uuid "e701c30a-31e0-5846-a5ac-4931665193d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437862} :answer-field/id #uuid "387cbfbf-b068-513c-93c4-71cb2f6085f2" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a88235e-0bbc-5c38-af1d-92f6a6eda26f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131361" :question/problem "![](images/17/1709a94b7361a1e8764d94199f9e84802ae925a09e6481250d0846b83bb6549f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- **III.** $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n- **IV.** $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/7c/7c831425eae7afd709d546a8e05100f495adc04bba6450cfa56d370b415a28f5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "5cb2647d-9528-5c30-ba8e-54caf973f4e8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481740}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c74d4b9-7ed4-529a-b818-13e80317f0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446560} {:answer/id #uuid "976ecb2e-4f04-5610-8207-c96b23772842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446561} {:answer/id #uuid "b06370cf-7aa1-54f8-9e46-dc3a42a8d19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446563} {:answer/id #uuid "c7df0803-65d5-5e82-a045-a191ec18b671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446564} {:answer/id #uuid "e214b4cf-1c30-5601-8ce0-ad80b4de52ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446567}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446560} :answer-field/id #uuid "b667cd59-08f8-5c6e-82c5-8b3ea70d6405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "40fa6adc-4679-5e13-a9fc-cb698f54e0e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105700" :question/problem "![](images/18/183ea541b33b8389de46f9ee1560ed1da2b999191477d7490eb4970922bdc43c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n\nII. $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n\nIII. $F$ is not irrotational\n\nIV. $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/b1/b1520791fcb3808604758b7d8f99443020d12075d4cc8bff78659880575b6ef2.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III and IV are false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational. And, since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ad78c5c9-ad7f-533a-a2ae-0ae1700b578f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481731 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221ac97f-6559-509a-a3d9-2450bf49633e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260172} {:answer/id #uuid "a2757385-050d-54a8-8369-31a9aa26112c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261289} {:answer/id #uuid "b7289054-f9d1-5ae6-bd2a-f55dcf3e02b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261483} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb179e1-20cb-58d0-b8da-a19ff4125371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262076} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd94cc3-eb96-53bb-8a15-64be7834efb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262088}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262076} :answer-field/id #uuid "8edf0788-7859-57f4-ac13-2b85b5bf5d5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b1f3451-5b4c-525b-84a2-e17f8516fc90" :question/math-academy-id "q-126547" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,i+({x}^{2}-y)\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)) \\\\  & =2x-2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c486f1f0-e8fa-5c16-9036-44a2ad1aee07" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f352f1a-1af2-5144-a245-10854ead8b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446554} {:answer/id #uuid "41e8b7d6-5c7a-5cf8-9c06-4d00a18513e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446557} {:answer/id #uuid "834c390d-d089-5913-89c4-08a44764f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446558} {:answer/id #uuid "dcefe323-f572-5c28-853b-0586c0615884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446565} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c44a28-6809-582c-b411-ffd64f99822a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446568}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446565} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc4e87e-69ab-505e-8ac2-a813c8707da5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "322bc9a1-81a0-53ce-920d-1108825e3484" :question/math-academy-id "q-131363" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5ac051f640c2b4eae760eee9c567ec35ce3169384ec2305554e21c6d2d587bbe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n\nII. $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n\nIII. $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n\nIV. $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/80/80c67e624f960147b7980ee90700be800a414ab1e2a2e47faf7e8821ee0c0623.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "dda57262-1ccc-5da1-bbd4-59647bd6412a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d194d4-bd3d-507f-8c59-044dbc7c81ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446555} {:answer/id #uuid "2afb3ad3-3542-5efb-92a8-0a844c4e01a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446556} {:answer/id #uuid "8854556c-9dbd-5890-ab92-8f478346b8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446559} {:answer/id #uuid "98578e86-a0e6-513f-9db5-c6b91293012b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446562} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f07f8f-3a33-5658-92ff-2b538d7dcca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446566}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446555} :answer-field/id #uuid "9999ac46-6f4e-593f-907b-51eaf6bed9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aedf76f0-5451-58bc-9366-057502daac94" :question/math-academy-id "q-126522" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(3x+2y)\\,i+({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x+2y) \\\\  & =-2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-2,1)} & =-2(-2)-2 \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f9980b60-ddef-518f-9c60-dd36d6feb6c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481731}}] :activity/title "The Curl of a Vector Field" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481564}]}
